<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Inner Asia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Inner Asian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Inner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> refers to the interior regions of Asia, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and the Central Asian steppe. It was the home of nomadic empires such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a> both bridged Chinese and Inner Asian political traditions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inner-asia.jpg" alt="Inner Asia" width="320" height="172" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eric Thrift · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Inner_Asia_(SRI).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
